  "external_suppliers": {
    "def-axiom-of-choice": {
      "id": "def-axiom-of-choice",
      "path": "items/def-axiom-of-choice.md",
      "status": "published",
      "domain": "mathematics",
      "kind": "definition",
      "exact_statement": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
      "sha256": "9c045248c142fcf687fdc3c5d25d8b3890ac08d1eca8b4e6d71bb00a4eb6ff58",
      "reading_status": "status/statement extracted; actual proof-reading extent belongs to specialist supplier record",
      "consumers": [
        "def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state",
        "lem-emg-smooth-geometric-causal-research-suppliers"
      ],
      "home_kind": "canonical-root-item",
      "original_page_homes": [
        {
          "path": "library/foundations/relations-functions-and-quotients.md",
          "page": "relations-functions-and-quotients",
          "is_B": false,
          "status": "published"
        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus": {
      "id": "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus",
      "path": "items/thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus.md",
      "status": "published",
      "domain": "mathematics",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $n\\ge1$\nand let $e_k(x)=\\exp(2\\pi i\\,k\\cdot x)$, $k\\in\\mathbb Z^n$, be the coordinate\ncharacters on $\\mathbb T^n$\n([[def-the-one-dimensional-torus-and-normalized-haar-integral]],\n[[def-fourier-coefficients-and-trigonometric-polynomials]]). Then:\n\n1. $(e_k)_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^n}$ is an orthonormal family in the complex Hilbert\n   space $L^2(\\mathbb T^n)$;\n2. it is an orthonormal basis: its closed linear span is $L^2(\\mathbb T^n)$;\n3. consequently the Fourier expansion\n   $f=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^n}\\widehat f(k)e_k$ and Parseval's identity\n   $$\\|f\\|_2^2=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^n}|\\widehat f(k)|^2,\\qquad \\langle f,g\\rangle=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^n}\\widehat f(k)\\overline{\\widehat g(k)}$$\n   hold for all $f,g\\in L^2(\\mathbb T^n)$, in the finite-subset-net sense; and\n4. the Fourier coefficient map is a surjective linear isometry of\n   $L^2(\\mathbb T^n)$ onto $\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z^n,\\mathbb C)$.\n\nNo Hilbert tensor-product identification is used anywhere.",
      "sha256": "6d017415a6b810647e7c94b874eeaf15f46699e548ad6957fc16aad60600d968",
      "reading_status": "status/statement extracted; actual proof-reading extent belongs to specialist supplier record",
      "consumers": [
        "def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state"
      ],
      "home_kind": "canonical-root-item",
      "original_page_homes": [
        {
          "path": "library/functional-analysis/orthonormal-bases-parseval-and-fourier-series.md",
          "page": "orthonormal-bases-parseval-and-fourier-series",
          "is_B": false,
          "status": "published"
        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem": {
      "id": "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem",
      "path": "items/thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem.md",
      "status": "published",
      "domain": "mathematics",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "exact_statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be\n$C^1$, and let $a\\in U$.  If $Df(a)$ is invertible, then there are open sets\n$V,W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $a\\in V\\subseteq U$ and $f(a)\\in W$ such that\n$f|_V:V\\to W$ is bijective.  Its inverse $g:W\\to V$ is $C^1$, and\n\n$$Dg(y)=Df(g(y))^{-1}\\qquad(y\\in W).$$\n\nThus $f$ is a local diffeomorphism at $a$.",
      "sha256": "6bfff112618fce00dc5c29b731ba87698c898bdaa448d7bc255afdd07689e108",
      "reading_status": "status/statement extracted; actual proof-reading extent belongs to specialist supplier record",
      "consumers": [
