    "def-principal-g-bundle-and-associated-fiber-bundle": {
      "id": "def-principal-g-bundle-and-associated-fiber-bundle",
      "path": "items/def-principal-g-bundle-and-associated-fiber-bundle.md",
      "status": "published",
      "domain": "mathematics",
      "kind": "definition",
      "exact_statement": "Let $G$ be a topological group, as in [[def-topological-group]], with identity $1$. A **right principal $G$-bundle** is a continuous map $\\pi:P\\to B$ with a continuous right action $(p,g)\\mapsto pg$, satisfying $p1=p$, $(pg)h=p(gh)$ and $\\pi(pg)=\\pi(p)$, together with equivariant bundle charts $\\theta_i:\\pi^{-1}(U_i)\\cong U_i\\times G$ over an open cover. Equivariance means $\\theta_i(pg)=(b,ag)$ whenever $\\theta_i(p)=(b,a)$. Charts use ordinary products as in [[def-locally-trivial-fiber-bundle]]. In each fiber the action is free and transitive: right multiplication on $G$ has those properties and the chart identifies the actions. Freeness alone is not the definition.\n\nA **left $G$-space** is a topological space $F$ with a continuous map $(g,x)\\mapsto gx$ such that $1x=x$ and $g(hx)=(gh)x$. Effectiveness of this action is not required. Define\n$$P\\times_G F=(P\\times F)/\\bigl((pg,x)\\sim(p,gx)\\bigr)$$\nwith the ordinary quotient topology, and write $[p,x]$ for its points. Precisely, the relation is the orbit relation of the right action $(p,x)\\cdot g=(pg,g^{-1}x)$. Its action law is $((p,x)\\cdot g)\\cdot h=(pgh,h^{-1}g^{-1}x)=(p,x)\\cdot(gh)$, and its generating relations are exactly the displayed ones. Thus the equivalence relation and quotient are defined without choosing orbit representatives.\n\nThe projection $r([p,x])=\\pi(p)$ is well-defined and continuous by [[thm-quotient-universal-property]], since $(p,x)\\mapsto\\pi(p)$ is continuous and constant on every orbit. This is the **associated fiber-bundle construction**; the next proposition proves its local triviality and pullback property. The quotient construction and projection already make sense before that proof.\n\nIf $B$ is empty then $P$ is empty. If $F$ is empty the associated space is empty even for nonempty $B$; this is allowed by our bundle convention. For singleton $F$ the construction is the orbit projection of the principal bundle. For the trivial group it is the product with $F$ over the chart-identified base. No AC is used.",
      "sha256": "c4c3153bd35a31b530d48007699891b271250cced5c054369fda4a2c35e48960",
      "reading_status": "status/statement extracted; actual proof-reading extent belongs to specialist supplier record",
      "consumers": [
        "def-emg-local-potentials-and-circle-data"
      ],
      "home_kind": "canonical-root-item",
      "original_page_homes": [
        {
          "path": "library/algebraic-topology/fibrations-fiber-bundles-and-homotopy-exact-sequences.md",
          "page": "fibrations-fiber-bundles-and-homotopy-exact-sequences",
          "is_B": false,
          "status": "published"
        }
      ]
    },
    "def-de-rham-cohomology": {
      "id": "def-de-rham-cohomology",
      "path": "items/def-de-rham-cohomology.md",
      "status": "published",
      "domain": "mathematics",
      "kind": "definition",
      "exact_statement": "The real **de Rham cohomology** is $H^k_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)=Z^k(M)/B^k(M)$, with $Z^k,B^k$ as in [[def-closed-and-exact-differential-forms]].\n\nThis is [[def-cohomology-object-of-a-cochain-complex]] in real vector spaces. Only a closed form $\\omega$ represents a class $[\\omega]$. For closed forms $\\omega,\\omega\\prime$, equality $[\\omega]=[\\omega\\prime]$ means precisely $\\omega\\prime-\\omega=d\\eta$ for some $(k-1)$-form $\\eta$. Addition and real scalar multiplication are induced by those of forms. All groups on the empty manifold are zero.",
      "sha256": "e390a5162791040b3d10742209d9b395f39cdab549c0e59fc7c41ec4c753cfdd",
      "reading_status": "status/statement extracted; actual proof-reading extent belongs to specialist supplier record",
      "consumers": [
        "def-emg-local-potentials-and-circle-data"
      ],
      "home_kind": "canonical-root-item",
      "original_page_homes": [
        {
          "path": "library/differential-geometry/the-de-rham-complex-homotopy-and-mayer-vietoris.md",
          "page": "the-de-rham-complex-homotopy-and-mayer-vietoris",
          "is_B": false,
          "status": "published"
        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-stokes-theorem-for-smooth-singular-chains": {
